Loops & Handlers

Today I learned about Loops & Handlers in Ansible, which help in writing efficient and event-driven automation ⚙️
Instead of repeating the same task multiple times, we can use loops.
 And instead of restarting services again and again, we use handlers smartly 🔁

🔹 🔄 loop & with_items
Loops allow us to run the same task for multiple items without duplication.
Example:
- name: Install multiple packages
 apt:
 name: "{{ item }}"
 state: present
 loop:
 - nginx
 - git
 - curl
Earlier with_items was used, but now loop is the modern and recommended approach.
 It makes Playbooks cleaner and more scalable 🚀

🔹 🔔 notify
The notify keyword is used to trigger handlers only when a task makes a change.
Example:
- name: Update config file
 copy:
 src: app.conf
 dest: /etc/app.conf
 notify: Restart service
This ensures actions are executed only when necessary, saving time and resources.

🔹 🛠️ Handlers Usage
Handlers are special tasks that run only when notified by other tasks.
 They are defined under the handlers section in a Playbook.
Example:
handlers:
 - name: Restart service
 service:
 name: nginx
 state: restarted
Handlers do not run unless triggered, making automation more efficient ⚡

🔹 🔁 Restart Services (Real Use Case)
In real-world automation:
 • Update configuration file
 • Notify handler
 • Restart service only if changes occur
This avoids unnecessary service restarts and improves system stability 🔐
Handlers are widely used in production-grade Playbooks.

🔥 3-Line Takeaway
Loops reduce repetition in tasks.
 Notify triggers handlers only on change.
 Handlers ensure smart and efficient service restarts.
